  "candidate": "k",
  "heard": "Even with the units aligned, the domains split out, and the probabilities verified, perplexity still grades the whole stream of text. A hard question often has just one spot where the answer matters. Standardized exams put that spot at the center. A known answer, automatic grading, and questions across dozens of subjects. That gives us a different ruler. For a while, the ruler gave us clear steps upward, but as the strongest models crowded toward the top, the gaps became harder to read. That's benchmark saturation.",
